Design recap:
```dart
/// The default write-time window (gh-1590): ...
const Duration ledgerSnapshotPersistInterval = Duration(seconds: 30);

final class LedgerSnapshotThrottle {
  LedgerSnapshotThrottle({
    this.minInterval = ledgerSnapshotPersistInterval,
    DateTime Function()? clock,
    void Function()? onFlushDue,
  }) : ...;
  bool shouldPersistNow();
  bool poll();
  void confirmPersisted();
  void reset();
  void dispose();
  bool get hasPending;
}
```

Tests:

1. "the first snapshot persists; N mutations inside the window coalesce":
```dart
var now = DateTime(2026, 10, 13, 12);
final throttle = LedgerSnapshotThrottle(minInterval: const Duration(seconds: 30), clock: () => now);
expect(throttle.shouldPersistNow(), isTrue);
throttle.confirmPersisted();
for (var i = 0; i < 49; i++) {
  now = now.add(const Duration(seconds: 1));
  expect(throttle.shouldPersistNow(), isFalse);
}
expect(throttle.hasPending, isTrue);
// 49 rapid mutations would have appended 49 snapshots before (gh-1590);
// they coalesce behind the trailing flush.
```

2. "the trailing flush becomes due one window after the first refused mutation; the next window re-opens":
```dart
... continue: now += 30s → expect(throttle.poll(), isTrue) → (caller persists) throttle.confirmPersisted();
expect(throttle.poll(), isFalse);
// window closed again immediately after the flush
expect(throttle.shouldPersistNow(), isFalse);
```

Wait — after poll() true and confirmPersisted, `shouldPersistNow` immediately after: now - lastPersistAt = 0 < 30s → false. ✓

3. "a mutation after the full window persists immediately (steady state ≈ one snapshot per window)":
```dart
final first = throttle.shouldPersistNow() → true; confirm.
now += 30s → expect(throttle.shouldPersistNow(), isTrue) (no pending needed); confirm.
```

4. "reset re-opens the window (fresh session)": after refused state, reset → shouldPersistNow true.

5. "dispose cancels the trailing leg" — hard to test without real timers... The timer fires onFlushDue after minInterval of REAL time. I could test with a tiny real interval (e.g., 20ms): arm by refusing, dispose, wait 50ms, expect flush callback NOT called. That's a real-timer test — acceptable (short). Also a production-leg test: small interval, refuse → wait ~3× interval → onFlushDue called once. Hmm, keep it light: one test with 30ms interval verifying the flush callback fires, and dispose prevents it.

